SELECT '2f3cbdab-36f4-5e9b-b491-a4fcfe496a81'::uuid, 'JF Effect | Muscle Foundation — Beginner — 3 days/week', 'block', 'bodybuilding', 'Hypertrophy', ARRAY['JF Effect', 'Men', 'Muscle', 'Hypertrophy', 'Beginner', 'Gym', '3 days', 'Full Body']::text[], 'Published', 12, 3,
  55, 'Build muscle and general strength with full-body training', $jf$Your first proper muscle-building program. Three full-body workouts a week using machines, cables and dumbbells that are easy to learn and easy to progress: when you hit the top of the rep range on every set, add a little weight. Two hard sets per exercise, an easier week every fourth week, and a rep-PR week at the end to see how far you've come. About 45–60 minutes. Optional: 2 easy 15–30 minute walks or bike rides a week.$jf$, $jf$Purpose: competent full-body training, sustainable muscle gain, general strength. Gender label is only a browsing tag.
Weekly sets (approx.): quads 7, chest 6, back 7, hamstrings 4 + hip thrust, side delts 2, arms 2+2, calves 2. Beginner range is 6–10 effective sets per muscle.
Double progression: when every set hits the top of the range at the target RIR, add the smallest increment next time.
Weeks 4 and 8 are lighter weeks (beginner version of a deload): 3-set exercises drop a set and everything is at 3–4 RIR; 2-set work stays 2 sets. Beginners carry little fatigue, but the easier week keeps technique sharp and adherence high.
Goblet squat → belt squat or back squat when the dumbbell gets too light for 12 hard reps.
Trim first if over 60 min: Day 1 plank, Day 3 dead bug, then a lateral raise or curl set.
Advance on consistent technique, completion and stable progress, not a calendar date.$jf$,
