SELECT 'ce5ba49b-7172-59dd-8623-879c00872982'::uuid, 'JF Effect | Muscle & Strength — Intermediate — 4 days/week', 'block', 'bodybuilding', 'Hypertrophy', ARRAY['JF Effect', 'Men', 'Muscle', 'Hypertrophy', 'Strength', 'Intermediate', 'Gym', '4 days', 'Upper Lower']::text[], 'Published', 12, 4,
  65, 'Build muscle with enough heavy compound work to keep getting stronger', $jf$Upper/lower, 4 days a week: two upper and two lower sessions built around a heavy squat, bench, incline press and Romanian deadlift, with the machine and cable work that actually grows muscle. Reps first, then load. Each block gets a little harder, with an easier week every fourth week, and the last block moves the big lifts into heavier rep ranges. About 55–75 minutes.$jf$, $jf$Purpose: productive upper/lower hypertrophy with enough compound strength practice. Not a six-day marathon.
Weekly sets (approx.): chest 8, back 10, side delts 6, rear delts 2, quads 8, hamstrings 8, glutes 2 direct + compounds, calves 3, arms 2–4 each.
Squat and bench run on RPE (6–8 reps → 5–7 → 4–6 by block). Everything else is double progression at the target RIR.
Squat: competition, high-bar or safety bar, whichever the client trains best; no powerlifting commands needed.
More arm work only by trading low-priority sets (e.g. one pec-deck or adductor set), never by stacking exercises.
Trim first if over 75 min: adductor, cable crunch, then one set of the reverse pec deck.
Changes from the JF Effect master spec (Nov 13 2026), and why:
- Upper B: the seated dumbbell shoulder press is replaced by a dumbbell lateral raise (3x12–20). Front delts already get about 6 hard pressing sets a week from the incline press and bench, while side delts were getting only 3 direct sets; the raise grows the side delts with less joint and recovery cost. Put the press back for clients who want it (swap note on the row).$jf$,
