SELECT 'f6125bc9-966d-5886-aed6-b35123edc9ba'::uuid, 'JF Effect | Glute & Physique Builder — Intermediate — 4 days/week', 'block', 'bodybuilding', 'Hypertrophy', ARRAY['JF Effect', 'Women', 'Glutes', 'Hypertrophy', 'Intermediate', 'Gym', '4 days', 'Upper Lower']::text[], 'Published', 12, 4,
  65, 'Build glutes and legs while training back, shoulders, chest and arms', $jf$Two lower days and two upper days. Lower days are built around the hip thrust, Romanian deadlift, split squats and a heavy squat, with abduction and hamstring work; upper days train your back, shoulders, chest and arms so the whole physique comes along. Each block gets a little harder, with an easier week every fourth week. About 55–75 minutes.$jf$, $jf$Purpose: glutes and legs first, with real back, shoulder, chest and arm training.
Glute volume: hip thrust 3 (4 in Blocks 2–3) + abduction 4 direct, with RDL, split squat, belt squat and lunge counted as partial overlap ≈ 12–14 effective glute sets a week. Hamstrings 7, quads 7, back 8, delts 6, chest 4.
If hip thrust / RDL / split-squat fatigue gets excessive, trim sets before adding exercises (first the calf raise, then one abduction set).
Trim first if over 75 min: standing calf raise (optional), cable crunch, then one set of the leg extension.
Changes from the JF Effect master spec (Nov 13 2026), and why:
- Lower B: the leg press (second bilateral quad compound right after the belt squat) is replaced by a lying hamstring curl (2x10–15). Hamstrings were the only lower-body muscle under ~6 weekly sets, while quads were still covered by the belt squat, reverse lunge and leg extension.
- Hip thrust gets a 4th set in Blocks 2 and 3: the glutes are this program's priority, so they get the volume progression.$jf$,
